From 02708d45e08265bee39c48dd56d8899fd66ca404 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexis Williams <alexis@typedr.at>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 19:00:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] tests: Skip what WebAssembly can't do, and fix what it
 showed up

Where an expectation can't hold on WebAssembly, skip the subtest, or
the part of it, with the reason:
- musl takes any locale name but has no locale data: collate's lists
  that sort differently under en_US, option-context's decimal comma,
  date's Polish month names, and gdatetime's en_GB formats, de_DE month
  names and eras.
- WasmFS has no sockets, and Emscripten can't spawn or fork processes,
  nor parse an IPv6 scope ID or list network interfaces.
- WasmFS keeps file times as a double count of milliseconds, and checks
  no directory's search permission.
- Time zones come from Intl, so no TZif file is read by path.
- gio-tool's launch command needs desktop applications and spawning,
  and WebAssembly's content types know no MIME types.

Where musl's output is right but differs, expect it: strftime() pads
years to four digits and has no %k, %l or %P, and iconv() reports a
character the target lacks once it has consumed the input. A
WebAssembly GMainContext polls no wakeup fd unless it is ownerless, so
mainloop expects one fewer fd.

Fix the tests themselves where they relied on more than they should:
- filenamecompleter connected g_main_loop_quit() to a signal with
  another signature, which traps, and expected completions in the order
  one file system lists them;
- gdbus-peer's credentials test assumed credentials on every Unix;
- socket-listener's mocks and messages-low-memory's malloc() look up the
  real function with dlsym(RTLD_NEXT), which finds nothing in a static
  WebAssembly link;
- test-printf's reader expected a pipe read to block;
- glib/tests never told the Python tests to run their programs with
  Node, and those tests expected abort() to end Node with SIGABRT.

Two workarounds keep tests running under Node: mainloop's nfds test
gives its file a byte, since WasmFS's poll() only takes a non-empty
file for readable, and fileutils' stdio-wrappers test works in a
temporary directory, since chmod() doesn't reach a host directory.

Only test sources and test build files change.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Wasm-specific]
